    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_ff61a88e40be48097de8eac400c540eca944b7d1cb031177fc4f1da657bc385c",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The reported run separates a costly main root-counting computation from a much cheaper production of Turing-method data: on the stated system the former used 24 hours 24 minutes of dedicated CPU time, while the selected twenty-point calculation took seconds. Recording program roles, compiler, hardware, and numeric capacities makes this asymmetry interpretable and reproducible within its historical regime, while the admitted readability and optimization choices prevent treating the timings as intrinsic costs of the mathematics.",
