    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_48512d637191f71ddb3dd07f0a782a314c8aae278281595e2c681b6995901040",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Riemann-Siegel's square-root-length main sum expands the feasible search range relative to the order-t comparison method, but fixed-precision representation, phase reduction, and accumulated summation error create a height-dependent reliability boundary. Precomputation can amortize the main-sum cost across many nearby evaluations, whereas a single evaluation retains the Dirichlet-sum bottleneck, so throughput gains and precision gains address different frontiers.",
